pis arvensis</italic> L.</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">uv-g</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">1324</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1"><italic>Verbascum densiflorum</italic> Bertol.</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">uv-g</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0</td></tr></tbody></table><table-wrap-foot><fn id="nt102"><p>The 63 plant species included all flower during this period (June and/or July) according to published phenological data <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="pone.0000556-Rauh1">&#x0005b;29&#x0005d;</xref>, and are divided into six bee-subjective colour categories (uv&#x0200a;&#x0003d;&#x0200a;ultraviolet, uv-b&#x0200a;&#x0003d;&#x0200a;uv-blue, b&#x0200a;&#x0003d;&#x0200a;blue, b-g&#x0200a;&#x0003d;&#x0200a;blue-green, g&#x0200a;&#x0003d;&#x0200a;green and uv-g&#x0200a;&#x0003d;&#x0200a;uv-green). Data presented are mean amounts of sugar produced (&#x000b5;g in 24 hours) per species, averaged across at least 30 flowers per species <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="pone.0000556-Chittka3">&#x0005b;17&#x0005d;</xref>, <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="pone.0000556-Raine2">&#x0005b;27&#x0005d;</xref>.</p></fn></table-wrap-foot></table-wrap><table-wrap id="pone-0000556-t003" position="float"><object-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1371/journal.pone.0000556.t003</object-id><label>Table 3</label><caption><title>Variation in nectar foraging performance for nine bumblebee colonies.</title></caption><graphic id="pone-0000556-t003-3" xlink:href="pone.0000556.t003"/><table frame="hsides" rules="groups" alternate-form-of="pone-0000556-t003-3"><colgroup span="1"><col align="left" span="1"/><col align="center" span="1"/><col align="center" span="1"/><col align="center" span="1"/><col align="center" span="1"/></colgroup><thead><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">colony</td><td colspan="4" align="left" rowspan="1">foraging rate (mg/hr)</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1"/><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1"